AB  - The aim of this study was to compare the (V)over dotO(2) kinetics parameters during maximal and supramaximal running exercises in sprinters (SPR) and middle-distance runners (MDR). Twelve SPR (age 20.6 +/- 3.4 years; and body mass 74.9 +/- 6.2 kg) and 10 MDR (age 17.6 +/- 1.4 years; and body mass 70.1 +/- 11.8 kg) performed, on different days, a maximal incremental running test for determination of the velocity at maximum oxygen uptake (v(V)over dotO(2)max) and 2 constant-speed tests for analysis of (V)over dotO(2) kinetics at 100 and 120% of v(V)over dotO(2)max. The MDR presented significantly higher values for the critical speed (18.0 +/- 1.0 vs. 14.2 +/- 1.0 km.h(-1)), v(V)over dotO(2)max (19.4 +/- 0.7 vs. 17.2 +/- 0.8 km.h(-1)), and time to exhaustion at 100% (437 +/- 53 vs. 366 +/- 49 seconds) than the SPR (p <= 0.05). However, the SPR demonstrated greater values for the anaerobic distance capacity (281.3 +/- 66.1 vs. 208.0 +/- 43.4 m). Although the mean response time and the time to attain the (V)over dotO(2)max of the (V)over dotO(2) kinetics was higher in the maximal than supramaximal running speed (p <= 0.05), no difference was found in these parameters as well for (V)over dotO(2) slow component (p > 0.05) between the groups. This study showed that different aerobic and anaerobic characteristics in MDR and SPR did not affect the (V)over dotO(2) kinetics in maximal and supramaximal intensities within the severe-intensity domain. From a practical perspective, parameters of (V)over dotO(2) response may provide helpful information for training control as the duration of stimulus close to (V)over dotO(2)max.
