AB  - This article presents the results of a study aimed at developing a training methodology based on steady, continuous long-distance running to restore the physical fitness and performance of wounded Airborne troops. This methodology aims to facilitate their rapid return to the combat zone and to scientifically substantiate its effectiveness. A three-week training program was developed, including warm-up, base, and warm-up microcycles aimed at gradually increasing the volume and intensity of running loads, developing overall endurance, speed, and sensory abilities. A distinctive feature of the program is the individualization of the training process based on monitoring heart rate variability. The effectiveness of the developed program was experimentally proven, as evidenced by statistically significant improvements in the morphofunctional indicators of physical performance and physical fitness of the experimental group. Analysis of heart rate variability dynamics demonstrated the program's ability to ensure an optimal level of adaptive capacity and prevent the development of regulatory system overstrain. The results obtained can be used to improve the system of physical rehabilitation of military personnel and increase the combat readiness of Airborne Forces units.
