AB  - We examined 26 professional riders during time trial (TT) competitions of the Grand Tours of cycling (Tour de France and Vuelta Espana; 1997-2003) for the exertional characteristics of contending vs. non-contending (i.e., support) riders. We categorized HR time during TT into training impulse (TRIMP) defined from seasonal VO(2max) testing [Phase I (< ventilatory threshold (VT approximate to 64% VO(2max)); Phase II (VT - respiratory compensation threshold, approximate to 83% VO(2max)); Phase III >RCP]. Races were: Short TT (<15km; 8.9 +/- 2.9km); Individual TT (>15km; 48.12 +/- 8.7km); Uphill TT (20.0 +/- 8.7 km) and Team TT (44.1 +/- 20.9 km). We observed statistically significant event-by-contender interactions for all TT (all, P<0.0001) except the short TT. During uphill TT, contenders exerted fewer total TRIMP (P<0.01), more Zone 3 TRIMP(P<0.05), and fewer Zone 2 TRIMP (P<0.01) vs. non-contenders. For individual TT, contenders accumulated more Total and Zone 3 TRIMP vs. non-contenders (all, P<0.05). Interestingly, during the team TT, contenders accumulated more Zone 3, and fewer Zone 2 TRIMP (all, P<0.05), despite having the opportunity to draft behind other riders while in paceline race formation. During TT events, contending riders compete at a level of exertion corresponding to a higher metabolic demand during the uphill TT, individual TT and team TT.
