KW  - EXPOSURE
AB  - Previous research indicates that exposure to nature brings psychological and physiological benefits to humans. Virtual reality (VR) technologies' rapid development provides fully immersive experiences for virtual restorative environment (VRE) users. However, stimulated environments' effects on subjective vitality and physical health need further investigation. Thus, this study examined VREs' psychological and physiological impacts on subjective vitality, attention restoration, landscape preference, and cardiovascular health. Using VR technologies, five landscape types-stream, urban forest, cycling path park, urban park, and urban environment-were analyzed for their differential effects. Data from 111 participants revealed significant variations in perceived vitality, psychological responses, and preferences across landscapes. One-way ANOVA tests demonstrated VR landscapes' significant effects on subjective vitality and restorativeness. Mann-Whitney U tests showed significant differences in landscape preference and pleasure. Correlation and pathway analyses highlighted direct and indirect relationships between restorativeness, landscape preference, and subjective vitality, suggesting perceived environmental qualities' substantial contribution to emotional well-being. Urban forest ranked the highest in perceived vitality, followed by urban park and stream; cycling path park had a negative impact on vitality compared to urban environment. Attention restoration, landscape preference, and pleasure were considerably higher in natural landscapes than in urban environments, supporting the attention restoration theory, stress reduction theory, and biophilia hypothesis. Indirect effects mediated by restorativeness and landscape preference accentuated environmental appraisal's influence on psychological benefits. Physiological responses, measured through heart rate variability, showed no significant differences among landscape types. These findings emphasize VREs' potential of promoting psychological well-being and offer insights into designing VREs and real-world environments.
