AB  - PurposeTraining zones are generally assessed by gas-exchange thresholds (GET). Several mathematical analyses of heart rate variability (HRV) are proposed for indirect GET determination. Our study aimed to investigate the accordance of the detrend fluctuation analysis (DFA alpha 1) for determining GET with first (VT1) and second ventilatory (VT2) thresholds in well-trained subjects.MethodsEighteen female and 38 male sub-elite cyclists performed a maximal incremental cycling test of 2-min stage duration with continuous gas exchange and HR measurements. Power output (PO), Oxygen uptake (V-center dot\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$$\dot{\text{V}}$$\end{document} O2) and HR at VT1 and VT2 were compared with DFA alpha 1 0.75 (HRVT1) and 0.50 (HRVT2). Agreements between PO, V-center dot\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$$\dot{\text{V}}$$\end{document} O2 and HR values were analyzed using Bland-Altman analysis.ResultsLarge limits of agreement between VT1 and HRVT1 were observed for measures of V-center dot\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$$\dot{\text{V}}$$\end{document} O2 expressed in mL.min-1.kg-1 [- 21.3; + 14.1], HR [ 39.2; + 26.9] bpm and PO [- 118; + 83] watts. Indeed, agreements were also low between VT2 and HRVT2 for measures of V-center dot\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$$\dot{\text{V}}$$\end{document} O2 [- 26.7; + 4.3] mL.min-1.kg-1, HR [- 45.5; + 10.6] bpm and PO [- 157; + 35] watts. Our results also showed a sex effect: women obtained worst predictions based on DFA alpha 1 than men for HR (p = 0.014), PO (p = 0.044) at VT1 and V(center dot)O2\documentclass[12pt]{minimal} \usepackage{amsmath} \usepackage{wasysym} \usepackage{amsfonts} \usepackage{amssymb} \usepackage{amsbsy} \usepackage{mathrsfs} \usepackage{upgreek} \setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt} \begin{document}$${\dot{\text{V}}\text{O}}_{2}$$\end{document}(p = 0.045), HR (p = 0.003) and PO (p = 0.004) at VT2.ConclusionThere was unsatisfactory agreement between the GET and DFA alpha 1 methods for VT1 and VT2 determination in both sex well-trained cyclists.Trial registration number 2233534 on 2024/03/05 retrospectively registered.ConclusionThere was unsatisfactory agreement between the GET and DFA alpha 1 methods for VT1 and VT2 determination in both sex well-trained cyclists.Trial registration number 2233534 on 2024/03/05 retrospectively registered.
