AB  - Nitrate (NO3-) supplementation resulting in higher plasma nitrite (NO2-) is reported to lower resting mean arterial blood pressure (MAP) and oxygen uptake (VO2) during submaximal exercise in non-athletic populations, whereas effects in general are absent in endurance-trained individuals. To test whether physiologic effects of NO3- supplementation depend on local muscular training status or cardiovascular fitness, male endurance-trained cyclists (CYC, n=9, VO2-max: 64 +/- 3mL/min/kg; mean +/- SD) and recreational active subjects serving as a control group (CON, n=8, 46 +/- 3mL/min/kg), acutely consumed nitrate-rich beetroot juice ([NO3-] similar to 9mmol) (NIT) or placebo (PLA) with assessment of resting MAP and energy expenditure during moderate intensity (similar to 50% VO2-max) and incremental leg cycling (LEG-ex) and arm-cranking exercise (ARM-ex). NIT increased (P<.001) resting plasma NO3- by similar to 1200% relative to PLA. Plasma NO2- increased similar to 25% (P<.01) with a significant change only in CYC. LEG-ex VO2 (similar to 2.60L/min), ARM-ex VO2 (similar to 1.14L/min), and resting MAP (similar to 87mm Hg) remained unchanged for CYC, and similarly for CON, no changes were observed for LEG-ex VO2 (similar to 2.03L/min), ARM-ex VO2 (similar to 1.06L/min), or resting MAP (similar to 85mm Hg). VO2-max was not affected by supplementation, but incremental test peak power was higher (P<.05) in LEG-ex for CYC in NIT relative to PLA (418 +/- 47 vs 407 +/- 46W). In both CYC and CON, high initial baseline values and small increases in plasma NO2- after NIT may have lowered the effect of the intervention implying that muscular and cardiovascular training status is likely not the only factors that influence the physiologic effects of NO3- supplementation.
