AB  - Downhill parts of a cycling race are among the most complex and stressful elements for cyclists. Virtual Reality (VR) is thus an interesting tool to monitor reactions to such situations, in safe environment. The present exploratory study aimed to measure physiological and subjective stress during a virtually-simulated downhill race, according to the degree of realism of the simulation. Eighteen young, healthy participants with various expertise in cycling (total amount of training hours) took part in the present experiment. They randomly performed 4 different conditions of immersion, by manipulating the position (sitting, SIT; on reclined Cycle Ergometer, ERG), with (VR) or without immersion (SCREEN), each time visualizing a virtual 5-min downhill cycling race from a personal point of view. Physiological stress responses were monitored with upper limb muscle activity (Electromyographic, EMG), electrodermal (Galvanic Skin Response, GSR) and heart activity (Electro-CardioGraphy, ECG). Subjective sensations of fear was assessed using visual analog scales. VR showed greater values than SCREEN conditions for most of the markers of stress. The condition that provided the greatest increase in physiological and subjective markers was ERG + VR. In this condition, a negative correlation was found between training hours (expertise) and values of stress. The more the athletes were trained, the less important the values were. These results demonstrate that VR induces psychophysiological stress which increases with the simulation's realism (from incongruent to congruent posture, for instance). The present study also showed that high expertise lowered the effect of VR.
