AB  - Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most common cardiac arrhythmia, affecting approximately 40 million individuals worldwide. In addition to traditional risk factors such as age, hypertension, and obesity, physical activity plays a crucial role. While moderate exercise exerts a protective effect, long-term high-intensity endurance training - particularly in middle-aged men - significantly increases the risk of AF. Mechanistically, enhanced vagal tone, atrial remodeling, inflammation, and fibrosis contribute to atrial electrical instability. Lifestyle modifications, including weight reduction and structured moderate exercise, can reduce AF risk and improve outcomes. Management follows current ESC guidelines, emphasizing rhythm and rate control, stroke prevention, and individualized exercise adjustment. In athletes, overtraining, doping, and bleeding risk under anticoagulation must be considered. Catheter ablation is often the preferred therapeutic strategy in active athletes.
