KW  - TRAVEL
AB  - Commuting is a significant source of stress in large cities, with its impact varying according to specific contextual factors. This study utilizes time-series stress data from wearable devices, combined with GPS tracking, to explore the spatiotemporal characteristics of commuting stress in Beijing. The analysis first visualizes commuting stress patterns across the pre-commute, in-commute, and post-commute periods. K-means clustering is then applied to identify four distinct stress patterns within the time-series data. Statistical tests, including ANOVA, chi-square tests, and logistic regression, are used to investigate the features of these spatiotemporal stress patterns and identify the key factors contributing to high-stress patterns. The results reveal that high-stress patterns are marked by an intense alarm phase, elevated peak stress levels, and prolonged recovery periods, posing a potential risk of mental exhaustion. Notably, while longer commute durations do not necessarily correlate with higher stress, long-distance commutes from suburban to urban areas, along with the crowded and complex environments of subways and e-bicycle, are strongly linked to increased stress levels. In light of Beijing's unique urban characteristics, this study recommends enhancing service quality and improving the perceived environment of public transportation and non-motorized vehicles during peak commuting hours. Furthermore, addressing transportation equity, particularly for low-income groups reliant on long-distance commutes from the suburbs to the city center, is crucial. Targeted interventions could help alleviate daily stress and enhance the well-being and quality of life of urban residents.
